            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Litegal1"
            },
            "reviewBody": "O.M.G. Seriously the *best* corn on the cob we have ever had! Sugar &amp; lemon juice? Insane! But it worked beautifully! I am somewhat of a corn snob (and would personally be perfectly content eatting it virtually raw from the stalk) so I was somewhat hesitant and made two pots - one just in boiling water, the other as you directed in your recipe. The subtle sweetness of the sugar meshed with the tangy lemon juice was superb. Thank you so much for recipe!"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Naomi Boyko"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Great for corn that is not very sweet or maybe a little past its prime. Also i like to add maybe a tsp. of salt. But, when corn is fresh and sweet I still prefer to cook it right in the husk in the microwave or on barbeque, cut off stem end and pull husk leaves and silk off from the top, no threads this way.",
            "datePublished": "2024-07-04T19:40:10.455Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "GiftedBasil5945"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I have been making corn using sugar and lemon juice for years now. Do NOT salt your water, it makes the corn mushy. I cook it a little different though and it comes out perfect every time. I put my corn in the cold water and bring to a boil. Let it cook for 10 minutes, turn heat off and let corn sit for an additional 10 minutes. Perfect corn every single time I make it.",
            "datePublished": "2023-07-15T13:33:27.887Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Mary Kimberly Cornell Olson"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I've made this a couple times. The only thing I did different was had my husband finish the cobs on the grill for that BBQ look and taste. So I boil for 8 minutes and then finish on the grill. Leftovers I cut the cob and fry it in a pan for nice change to go with a regular dinner.",
            "datePublished": "2020-06-27T20:59:54.023Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "SturdySeed2754"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Followed the recipe just added 3 minutes to the off burner boil….drained….added butter….corn was perfectly crisp and tasty….soggy corn on the cob is a thing of the past in my house….",
            "datePublished": "2023-02-16T23:37:30.748Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "kylieshipsky"
            },
            "reviewBody": "The best way to prepare corn on the cob that I've tried so far! And it was super easy. I had 4 ears (1 of them smaller than the others), so I cut the sugar down to 1 Tbsp and the lemon juice to 1/2 Tbsp, and it was perfect. I couldn't believe how tender the kernels turned out. Thank you for sharing your mother-in-law's recipe, Jamie!",
            "datePublished": "2021-09-14T11:16:09.933Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
